:title("Go (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses a Go subset and executes it directly, without generating code.

The subset: package and import clauses (parsed, ignored), functions with typed
parameters and multiple return values (func divmod(a int, b int) (int, int)), methods
with value receivers (func (p Point) manhattan() int), struct types with positional
composite literals (Point{3, 4}), field access and assignment, := short declarations
(also multiple: q, r := divmod(7, 2)), var declarations with zero values, parallel
assignment (a, b = b, a) and the blank identifier _, slices ([]int{...}, append, len,
indexing, element assignment), maps (map[K]V{...} literals and make(map[K]V); reads
of missing keys yield the zero value of the value type - int/string/bool -, m[k] = v,
len, delete(m, k), the comma ok idiom v, ok := m[k], and range over the entries),
function literals as values (func(x int) int { ... }) with real closures over their
scope, defer for plain and pkg.fn calls (arguments evaluate at defer time, the calls
run LIFO at function exit), the three for forms (classic, condition only, and
for i, v := range s - also over an int like range 5, Go 1.22, and over maps), switch
with subject
(case 2, 3: compares ==) and without (the first true case wins; no fallthrough, an
implicit break ends every case), Go style if/else without parentheses (else if works), break,
continue, i++ and i-- as statements, assignment operators += -= *= /= %=, fmt.Println
and fmt.Print, os.Exit, and string concatenation. int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates.

Also genuinely implemented (added while widening toward the full language, and
measured by tests/go-test-full.go): grouped declarations - several names sharing a
type in parameters, results, struct fields, var and const specs -, named results
with a bare return, variadic parameters and the s... spread, the full operator set
(bitwise and or xor and-not, shifts, the matching compound assignments, unary
complement), const blocks with iota and implicit repetition, every composite-literal
form (slice, array with counted or indexed elements, map, struct - positional or
keyed -, anonymous struct types, and untyped nested brace groups), make for maps and
slices, new, the conversion builtins, cap/copy/clear/min/max/append with many values,
call suffixes on any expression (func literals, elements, returned functions), slice
expressions, if and switch with an init statement, fallthrough, labelled statements
with labelled break and continue and goto, per-iteration loop variables (Go 1.22),
defer on any callee including function literals, interface declarations (dispatch is
dynamic, so satisfaction is structural by construction), type switches, the two-result
type assertion, value versus pointer method receivers, method values, embedded structs
with field promotion, and panic/recover with the defers of every unwound frame.

Widened surface (parsed so a real Go file gets through; -warn-unsupported downgrades
the abort to a warning and keeps the rest running, enough for call graphs/CFGs/traces):
channel and chan types are parsed in type positions. Accepted but reported not
implemented (no concurrency or channel model): goroutines (go f()), channel send
(ch <- v) and receive (<-ch), and select. Where operands exist (the goroutine callee,
the send/receive channel and value) they still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so
their calls stay visible.

Go strings are BYTE strings here too: len and s[i] count UTF-8 bytes, a string slice
s[lo:hi] is a byte range, ranging a string yields byte offsets and runes, and the
literal escapes are Go's own (the byte-wise x and octal forms, and the eight digit U
form).

Deviations and limits: structs behave like references (as if every value were a *T),
append mutates in place (write s = append(s, v) as usual), a bare-name composite
literal must not appear in an if/for/switch header (the bracketed forms may), map
iteration and printing follow the insertion order (real Go randomizes resp. sorts),
'var m map[K]V' is not a usable map (create maps with a literal or make). There is one
numeric type at RUNTIME, so a float and an int with the same value are
indistinguishable and integer division decides - except where a written-out conversion
makes the type known while the program is built: float64(i)/2 is 2.5 and a sized
unsigned type wraps to its width, so uint8(0xF0) complements to 0x0F. A pointer to a
plain variable IS modelled (and so is
new(T) of a scalar): it is a cell over the variable's scope slot, so *p = v, f(&n) and
**pp = v write through and p == &n holds. Every other &-form is the identity on the
value, since structs, slices and maps behave like references already.

An ARRAY is a plain host array and a SLICE is a header {a, o, n, c} over one, so the
two behave as Go says: an array is a VALUE (assigning it, or passing it, copies, and
the copy is deep), while s[1:3] shares the backing array, cap() answers something of
its own, s[:2:3] caps the capacity and append writes into the shared storage while it
fits. complex128 is a {re, im} box entered through an imaginary literal or complex().

Generics are ERASED: type parameter
lists, constraints and instantiations (f[int], T[K, V]) are parsed and ignored, which
behaves like the instantiated form in a dynamically typed value model.

The entry point is func main(); self checking programs end with os.Exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     = PackageClause { Import } { TypeDecl | TypeGroup | TopConst | FuncDecl | TopVar } ;

PackageClause = "package" KwEnd Id ;
Import      = "import" KwEnd ( ImportStr | "(" { ImportStr } ")" ) ;
ImportStr   <~~ resolveImport(up.in.slice(1, up.in.length - 1), up.pos) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7e" } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// type Point struct { x int; y int } and type Shape interface { Area() int }.
// The name is pushed first; the body decides between a struct (built) and an
// interface (accepted, then reported not implemented - no interface semantics).
TypeDecl    <~~ buildTypeDecl(takeAll(), up.pos) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ( StructBody | IfaceBody | DefinedType ) ;
// type myInt int / type Celsius float64: a defined type over an existing one. The
// value model has no distinct type for it, so the name becomes the CONVERSION to its
// underlying type - which is exactly how a defined type is used in an expression.
DefinedType <~~ push({under: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// Struct fields: one or more names sharing a type (x, y int), an optional raw
// string tag, and embedded fields (a bare type name, whose field name is the type).
StructBody  = "struct" KwEnd "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl   = NamedField | EmbedField ;
NamedField  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } SameLine FType [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FType       <~~ push({fty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An embedded field's NAME is its type name (w.base09 as well as the promoted w.id).
EmbedField  = [ "*" ] [ Id "." ] Id <~~ push(up.in); push({fty: ""}) ~~> [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FieldTag    = RawString <~~ pop() ~~> ;
IfaceBody   <~~ push({iface: true}) ~~>
            = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// A grouped type declaration (type ( A struct {...}; B int )): the balanced body is
// skipped and reported not implemented (its members are not built). Tried after
// TypeDecl, which needs an Id rather than '('.
TypeGroup   <~~ notImpl("grouped type declaration", up.pos) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd SkipGroup [ ";" ] ;

// const Name [Type] = expr is GENUINELY bound like a var (its value is reused). A
// grouped const block (const ( A = iota ... )) is skipped and reported not implemented
// (iota, implicit repetition and bit-shift expressions are not modeled).
// const ( A = iota; B; C ): every spec gets the running iota index, and a spec
// without its own '=' repeats the previous spec's expression list.
ConstGroup  <~~ push(makeConstGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd "(" { ConstSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
ConstSpec   <~~ push(makeConstSpec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
ConstSingle <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;

// func name(params) rets { } and func (r Type) name(params) rets { }
FuncDecl    <~~ buildFunc(takeAll()) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ RetTypes ] Block ;
// Generics are ERASED: a type parameter list is parsed and ignored, the constraint with
// it, and an instantiation (f[int], T[K, V]) is the identity on the value it indexes.
// The value model is dynamically typed, so an erased generic behaves exactly like the
// instantiated one - only compile-time type CHECKING is lost, which a syntax-driven
// front end does not do anyway.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParamGrp { "," TypeParamGrp } "]" ;
TypeParamGrp = Id { "," Id } Constraint ;
Constraint  = ConstrTerm { "|" ConstrTerm } ;
ConstrTerm  = [ "~" ] Type ;
// A POINTER receiver (func (c *T) m()) sees the caller's struct; a VALUE receiver
// (func (c T) m()) sees a copy, so mutations in the method do not reach the caller.
Receiver    <~~ push({recv: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "*" <~~ push({ptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ")" ;
// Parameters: several names may share a type (a, b int), a name may be omitted
// entirely (func(int) - only in type positions), and the last one may be variadic
// (vals ...int). A named group is tried first; on an unnamed list it fails and the
// bare-type form takes over.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ ParamItem { "," ParamItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ParamItem   = NamedParam | AnonParam ;
NamedParam  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ Variadic ] Type ;
AnonParam   = [ Variadic ] Type ;
Variadic    <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// Results: a single type, or a parenthesized list that may name its results
// (func f(a int) (q, r int)). Named results become zero-valued locals that a bare
// 'return' yields. NamedAhead decides which shape it is BEFORE any name is pushed,
// so the unnamed list never leaks half a name list onto the tag stack.
RetTypes    = NamedRets | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
NamedRets   <~~ push({rets: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NamedAhead "(" NamedRetGrp { "," NamedRetGrp } ")" ;
NamedRetGrp = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } Type ;
// A parenthesized list is NAMED exactly when some element at depth 1 is two words
// separated by whitespace ('q, r int' has 'r int'; '(int, string)' has no such pair).
NamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var depth = 0
        var cc = 0
        var prevWord = false
        var sawSpace = false
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { break }
            var isWord = (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { sawSpace = true; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 40) { depth++; prevWord = false; sawSpace = false; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                prevWord = false
                sawSpace = false
                i++
                continue
            }
            if (depth === 1 && prevWord && sawSpace && (isWord || cc === 91 || cc === 42)) { return undefined }
            prevWord = isWord
            sawSpace = false
            i++
        }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01unnamed-results", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A package level var, registered right away. Grouped (var ( a int; b = 2 )) runs each
// spec in order. A single const at top level is bound right away too.
TopVar      <~~ var t = pop(); t() ~~>
            = VarGroup | VarDecl ;
TopConst    <~~ var t = pop(); t() ~~>
            = ConstGroup | ConstSingle ;

Type        = "*" Type | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT | NamedType ;
// A named type: a predeclared name, a qualified one (time.Duration) or a generic
// instantiation (Stack[int]).
NamedType   = ( "int" KwEnd | "string" KwEnd | "bool" KwEnd | Id ) [ "." Id ] [ TypeArgs ] ;
TypeArgs    = "[" Type { "," Type } "]" ;
StructTypeT = "struct" KwEnd SkipBlock ;
// A pointer type (*T) is transparent (structs already behave like references) and a
// fixed size array ([N]T / [...]T) declares like a slice; the size is parsed, ignored.
ArrayTypeT  = "[" ( "..." | Expression <~~ pop() ~~> ) "]" Type ;
MapTypeT    = "map" KwEnd "[" Type "]" Type ;
FuncTypeT   = "func" KwEnd "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" [ Type | "(" Type { "," Type } ")" ] ;
// Channel and (anonymous) interface types are parsed so declarations mentioning
// them type-check syntactically; their values are only produced/consumed through
// constructs that are themselves reported as not implemented.
ChanTypeT   = ( "chan" KwEnd [ "<-" ] | "<-" "chan" KwEnd ) Type ;
IfaceTypeT  = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   = Block | If | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Defer | GoStmt | Select | Fallthrough
            | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | VarGroup | VarDecl | Labeled | SimpleStmt | Empty ;

// fallthrough: accepted, reported not implemented (our switch has no fallthrough model).
// fallthrough: a signal the enclosing switch body catches (see runSwitch).
Fallthrough <~~ push(makeFallthrough()) ~~>
            = "fallthrough" KwEnd [ ";" ] ;

// go f(args) / go pkg.Fn(args): a goroutine. True concurrency is not modeled, so
// it is reported not implemented; under -warn-unsupported the call still evaluates
// (its callee stays visible in call graphs). select likewise has no channel runtime.
GoStmt      <~~ push(makeGoStmt(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "go" KwEnd PostfixExpr [ ";" ] ;
// select { case v := <-ch: ...; case ch <- x: ...; default: ... }. The arms are tried
// in source order and the first READY one runs, which under the cooperative model is
// deterministic. An arm's body is a statement list, so it needs NotSelLabel to stop
// before the next 'case' / 'default' (both would otherwise parse as an identifier
// expression, exactly as they would in a switch body - which is why that one is
// parsed flat instead).
Select      <~~ push(makeSelect(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "select" KwEnd "{" { SelArm } "}" ;
SelArm      = SelRecv | SelSend | SelDefault ;
SelRecv     <~~ push(makeSelRecv(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelSend     <~~ push(makeSelSend(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelDefault  <~~ push(makeSelDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ":" SelBody ;
SelBody     <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { SelStmt } ;
SelStmt     = NotSelLabel Statement ;
NotSelLabel = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it (and over line
        // comments) before looking at the keyword.
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01end-of-file", 0) }
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 8192 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var w = ""
        while (i < 8192 && w.length < 8) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 97 || cc > 122) { break }
            w += String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
        }
        if (w === "case" || w === "default") { return abnf.newToken("\x01select-arm-label", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// defer f(args) / defer pkg.fn(args): the function value and the arguments are
// evaluated now, the call runs when the surrounding function returns (LIFO).
Defer       <~~ push(makeDefer(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "defer" KwEnd DeferTarget "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] ] ")" [ ";" ] ;
// Whatever the call is made ON: a name, a package member, a method value or a
// function literal. It is evaluated at defer time, together with the arguments.
// It must NOT swallow the call itself, so the chain carries only the non-call
// suffixes and the primary is restricted to the forms a callee can take.
DeferTarget <~~ push({dfn: pop()}) ~~>
            = DeferChain ;
DeferChain  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DeferPrim { FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
DeferPrim   = "(" Expression ")" | FuncLit | VarRef ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// var x int = 5 / var s string -- the zero value depends on the type.
VarDecl     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
VType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// var ( a int = 1; b = 2 ): each spec (one name) becomes a var-decl thunk; the group
// runs them in source order. Tried before VarDecl (needs '(' after var).
VarGroup    <~~ push(makeVarGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd "(" { VarSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
VarSpec     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;

// Short declarations, assignments, ++/-- and expression statements. The cores do
// not eat the separator themselves: for headers reuse them between the semicolons.
// MapOk (v, ok := m[k]) must come first: as a ShortDecl it would miss the flag.
SimpleStmt  = ( AssertOk | RecvOk | MapOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign | ChanSend | ExprOnly ) [ ";" ] ;

// ch <- v: a channel send. The left side is a postfix expression (so the leading
// '<' of a relational compare is never mistaken for the '<-' send token: a compare
// is written 'a < -b' with a space). Accepted, then reported not implemented; both
// operands still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so their calls stay visible.
// v, ok := <-ch: the received value and whether the channel was still OPEN. It
// must be tried before ShortDecl, which would destructure the value itself.
RecvOk      <~~ push(makeRecvOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              ( ":=" <~~ push({decl: true}) ~~> | "=" <~~ push({decl: false}) ~~> ) "<-" Expression ;
ChanSend    <~~ push(makeChanSend(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ;

// The two-result type assertion: v, ok := x.(T) yields the zero value and false
// instead of panicking. Its base must NOT take the .(T) suffix itself, so the chain
// here is the ordinary one minus TypeAssertSfx.
AssertOk    <~~ push(makeAssertOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ( ":=" | "=" )
              AssertBase "." "(" AssertType ")" ;
AssertBase  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | FieldSfx | SliceSfx | IndexSfx | CallSfx } ;
AssertType  <~~ push({at: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// The comma ok idiom: the value (or the zero value) plus the key presence flag.
MapOk       <~~ push(makeMapOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Primary "[" Expression "]" ;
ExprOnly    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

ShortDecl   <~~ push(makeShortDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ":=" ExprList ;

IncDecStmt  <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// a = e / a, b = b, a / p.x = e / s[i] = e / a += e
Assign      <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target { "," Target } AssignOp <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> ExprList ;
// Longest first: '&^=' before '&=', and the bare '=' last of all.
AssignOp    = "&^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | "=" ;

// A leading '*' is a pointer store: '*p = v' writes through p's cell, '**pp = v'
// through two of them. On a value that is not a pointer cell it stays transparent,
// which is what a struct pointer needs - those are already references.
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { "*" <~~ push({star: true}) ~~> } Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

ExprList    = Expression { "," Expression } ;

Empty       <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = ";" ;

// Go style headers without parentheses. Composite literals are gated off inside.
// 'if init; cond { }': the init statement is scoped to the whole if/else chain.
If          <~~ push(makeIfG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd CondOn [ InitAhead FInit ";" ] Expression CondOff Block [ "else" KwEnd ( If | Block ) ] ;

// ForCond is tried before ForClassic: a classic header would leave the
// composite literal gate pushed when it fails at its first semicolon.
// ForEver ('for {') comes first: the other forms would push the composite-literal
// gate and then fail on the '{', leaking the marker. Each form claims the pending
// label (see Labeled) at the 'for' keyword, BEFORE its body is built, so a nested
// loop cannot steal the outer loop's label.
For         = ForEver | ForRange | ForCond | ForClassic ;
ForEver     <~~ push(makeForEver(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> Block ;
// for i, v := range s / for _, v := range s / for i := range s / for range s
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~>
              [ Id <~~ push({rv: up.in}) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push({rv: up.in}) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "range" KwEnd CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;
ForClassic  <~~ push(makeForClassic(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~>
              CondOn [ FInit ] ";" [ FCond ] ";" [ FPost ] CondOff Block ;
// The names a ':=' init declares are recorded so a classic for can give each
// iteration its own copy of them (Go 1.22 loop-variable scoping).
FInit       <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop(), names: takeDeclNames()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
FCond       <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
FPost       <~~ push({k:"post", t:pop()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
SimpleStmt2 = AssertOk | RecvOk | MapOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign | ExprOnly ;
ForCond     <~~ push(makeWhileG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;

// Go switch: no fallthrough (an implicit break ends every case), several values per
// case, with a subject (compared ==) or without one (the first true condition wins).
Switch      = TypeSwitch | ValueSwitch ;
// A type switch (switch x.(type) / switch v := x.(type)) needs interface and type-set
// semantics we do not model: the subject (an identifier chain) is accepted and the
// body skipped, then it is reported not implemented. Tried before the value switch so
// its subject's '.(type)' is not mistaken for a value plus a type assertion.
// switch v := x.(type) { case int: ... }: the subject is evaluated once, every case
// lists TYPES (compared with the runtime type test), and the bound name takes the
// subject's value inside the matching case body.
TypeSwitch  <~~ push(makeTypeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd TypeSwAhead [ InitAhead FInit ";" ] [ Id <~~ push({tsname: up.in}) ~~> ":=" ]
              CondOn AssertBase CondOff "." "(" "type" KwEnd ")"
              "{" { TypeCase | Statement } "}" ;
TypeCase    <~~ push(makeTypeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd CaseType { "," CaseType }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;
CaseType    <~~ push({ct: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// The subjectless body is tried before the subject so a bare 'switch {' never enters
// SwitchSubj: that would push the composite-literal gate marker (CondOn) and then fail
// its Expression on the '{', leaking the marker (pushes are not rolled back) and
// blocking every later composite literal.
ValueSwitch <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd [ InitAhead FInit ";" ]
              ( "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}"
              | SwitchSubj "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}" ) ;
SwitchSubj  <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = CondOn Expression CondOff ;
// The body is parsed flat: the labels must be tried before Statement (a bare 'case'
// would parse as an identifier expression), and the builder groups the statements
// under the label they follow.
CaseLabel   <~~ push(makeCaseLabel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> { "," Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "return" KwEnd [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;

// break / continue carry an optional label; the labelled forms travel up as a
// signal until the loop that carries that label catches them.
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "break" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinueG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "continue" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "goto" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ";" ] ;

// 'name:' in front of a statement. LabelAhead decides before anything is pushed,
// so a short declaration ('x := 1', whose ':' is the head of ':=') never leaks a
// half-parsed label onto the tag stack.
// pendingLabel is set BEFORE the statement is built, so the loop builder inside
// can claim it (that is what makes 'continue outer' reach the right loop).
Labeled     <~~ var lt = pop(); push({lbl: pop(), t: lt}) ~~>
            = LabelAhead Id <~~ pendingLabel = up.in; push(up.in) ~~> ":" Statement ;
LabelAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it first.
        while (i < 4096) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        cc = c.peek(i)
        if (!((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95)) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-label", 0)
        }
        while (i < 4096) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        return (c.peek(i) === 58 && c.peek(i + 1) !== 61)
            ? undefined
            : abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-label", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
Expression  = OrExpr ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&&" EqExpr } ;

EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;

// Go's two binary levels below the comparisons: '+ - | ^' bind loosest, then
// '* / % << >> & &^'. The single-character '&' and '|' must not swallow the first
// half of '&&' / '||', which bind even looser - NotAmp/NotBar reject that.
AddExpr     <~~ push(goFold(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~>
              { SameLine AddOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> } ;
AddOp       = "+" | "-" | "^" | BarOp ;
BarOp       = "|" NotBar ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(goFold(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~>
              { SameLine MulOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> } ;
MulOp       = "*" | "/" | "%" | "<<" | ">>" | "&^" | AmpOp ;
AmpOp       = "&" NotAmp ;
NotAmp = :script(~~
    (c.peek(0) === 38) ? abnf.newToken("\x01logical-and", 0) : undefined
~~) ;
NotBar = :script(~~
    (c.peek(0) === 124) ? abnf.newToken("\x01logical-or", 0) : undefined
~~) ;

UnaryExpr   = Recv | Neg | Not | Compl | Plus | AddrOf | Deref | PostfixExpr ;
// ^x: the bitwise complement (Go spells it '^', not '~'). Unary '+' is the identity.
Compl       <~~ push(makeCompl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "^" UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> ;
Plus        = "+" UnaryExpr ;
// <-ch: a channel receive as a value. Accepted, reported not implemented; the
// operand still evaluates under -warn-unsupported. Tried before Neg so '<-' wins
// over '<' (a compare's '<' is followed by whitespace or a different next token).
Recv        <~~ push(makeRecv(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "<-" UnaryExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
// &x (address of) and *p (dereference). Taking the address of a plain NAME yields a
// pointer cell that reaches back into the variable's scope slot, which is what makes
// *p = v, bump(&n) and **pp = v write through to the variable. Every other &-form is
// still the identity on the value: structs, slices and maps already behave like
// references, so &Point{1, 2} and &s[i] need no cell. Binary * (multiply) is matched
// at MulExpr, so unary * only appears in an operand position.
AddrOf      = AddrVar | AddrAny ;
AddrVar     <~~ push(makeAddrVar(pop())) ~~>
            = "&" Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> NotSfxStart ;
AddrAny     = "&" UnaryExpr ;
// &name is only the variable form when the name is not the head of a composite
// literal, a call, a selector or an index. Anchored to the very NEXT byte, so it must
// be a script rather than a !"literal" lookahead (which would skip the whitespace).
NotSfxStart = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        if (cc === 123 || cc === 40 || cc === 46 || cc === 91) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01address-of-a-suffixed-operand", 0)
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;
Deref       <~~ push(makeDeref(pop())) ~~>
            = "*" UnaryExpr ;

// p.manhattan(), fmt.Println(...), p.x, s[i], and slice expressions s[lo:hi:max].
// A slice expression is accepted and reported not implemented; its base and every
// bound still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so nested calls stay visible. Tried
// before the plain postfix chain so the ':' inside the brackets is reached.
// A suffix chain over any primary: .m(args), .field, [i], [lo:hi:max], (args) and
// .(T). The plain call suffix is what makes func literals, slice elements and
// returned functions callable (func(){...}(), fs[0](), adder(3)(10)).
PostfixExpr = CallMember ;
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | FieldSfx | SliceSfx | IndexSfx | CallSfx | TypeAssertSfx } ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] ] ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
SliceSfx    <~~ push(makeSliceSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Expression <~~ push({lo: pop()}) ~~> ] ":" [ Expression <~~ push({hi: pop()}) ~~> ]
              [ ":" Expression <~~ push({mx: pop()}) ~~> ] "]" ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
CallSfx     <~~ push(makeCallSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] ] ")" ;
Spread      <~~ push({spread: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// x.(T): a type assertion, GENUINELY handled as the identity on the value (a
// successful assertion yields the same underlying value; the target type is parsed
// and ignored). It pushes no suffix, so foldCallMember passes the value through.
// Only the single-result form is supported (not the comma-ok v, ok := x.(T)).
TypeAssertSfx = "." "(" Type ")" ;

Primary     = "(" Expression ")" | ImagLit | DotFloatLit | HexFloatLit | FloatLit | HexLit | OctLit
            | BinLit | LegOctLit | DecULit | DqString | RawString | RuneLit
            | IntLit | True | False | Nil | FuncLit | MakeCall | NewCall | ConvExpr
            | CompositeLit | Call | Iota | VarRef ;

// ----- Composite literals -----
// One production covers every form: []T{...}, [N]T{...}, [...]T{...}, map[K]V{...},
// Name{...} (positional or keyed by field) and struct{...}{...}. An element may be a
// bare brace group ([][]int{{1}, {2, 3}}), which is built against the element type.
// Gated off inside if/for/switch headers, where '{' opens the body instead.
CompositeLit <~~ push(makeCompositeLit(takeAll())) ~~>
